<main id="main">

<section class="tc-hero"><div class="tc-w tc-hero-g"><h1 class="tc-h1"><span><i>Teach</i></span><span><i>what</i></span><span><i><em>lasts.</em></i></span></h1><div class="tc-hero-side"><p class="tc-ey">The craft of teaching</p><p class="tc-lead">Teacher is an independent guide for teachers, tutors and trainers. Clear, practical methods for planning lessons, explaining well, asking better questions and running a calm, focused room.</p><a class="tc-btn" href="#arc"><span>Explore the lesson arc</span><i>&darr;</i></a></div></div><figure class="tc-hero-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758270704587-43339a801396?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&h=700&q=75" alt="a professor lecturing to students in a lecture hall" width="1600" height="700" fetchpriority="high"></figure></section>

<div class="tc-mq mq" aria-hidden="true"><div><span>Explain</span><i>/</i><span>Model</span><i>/</i><span>Question</span><i>/</i><span>Practise</span><i>/</i><span>Feedback</span><i>/</i><span>Revisit</span><i>/</i><span>Explain</span><i>/</i><span>Model</span><i>/</i><span>Question</span><i>/</i><span>Practise</span><i>/</i><span>Feedback</span><i>/</i><span>Revisit</span><i>/</i></div></div>

<section class="tc-band"><div class="tc-band-i pxw"><img data-px=".12" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793592178816-388652cb4c7b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1800&h=900&q=75" alt="a school building with glowing windows at dusk" width="1800" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><div class="tc-w tc-band-g stg"><div class="rv"><b><span data-count="5">5</span><small>s</small></b><p>to wait after asking a question</p></div><div class="rv"><b><span data-count="3">3</span></b><p>parts to every good explanation</p></div><div class="rv"><b><span data-count="80">80</span><small>%</small></b><p>success rate to aim for in practice</p></div><div class="rv"><b><span data-count="1">1</span></b><p>clear objective per lesson</p></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-arc" id="arc"><div class="tc-arc-l"><div class="tc-arc-st"><figure class="tc-arc-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758270704021-361c165d68fd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&h=1100&q=75" alt="a teacher asking a question to students in a classroom" width="900" height="1100" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="tc-arc-p"><i id="arc-bar"></i></div></div></div><div class="tc-arc-r"><p class="tc-ey rv">The lesson arc</p><h2 class="rv">Five movements of a great lesson</h2><ol class="tc-mv"><li class="rv"><span>01</span><h3>Hook</h3><p>Start with a question, a puzzle or a picture that makes the class want the answer. Link it to what they already know.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>02</span><h3>Model</h3><p>Show, step by step, thinking aloud. Students see the expert process, not just the finished product.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>03</span><h3>Guided practice</h3><p>Do the next examples together, gradually handing over more of the thinking.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>04</span><h3>Independent practice</h3><p>Students work alone while you circulate, spot misconceptions and give quick feedback.</p></li><li class="rv"><span>05</span><h3>Check and close</h3><p>A short exit question shows who has it and who needs more tomorrow.</p></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="tc-s" id="planner"><div class="tc-w"><div class="tc-pl-h"><p class="tc-ey">Lesson planner</p><h2 class="rv">Shape your next lesson in seconds</h2></div><div class="tc-pl rv"><div class="tc-pl-f"><label for="lp-d">Lesson length<select id="lp-d"><option value="40">40 minutes</option><option value="50" selected>50 minutes</option><option value="60">60 minutes</option><option value="90">90 minutes</option></select></label><label for="lp-t">Lesson type<select id="lp-t"><option value="0">New concept</option><option value="1">Practice and consolidation</option><option value="2">Revision before a test</option><option value="3">Practical or project</option></select></label></div><div class="tc-tl" id="lp-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div><ol class="tc-tl-l" id="lp-o" aria-live="polite"></ol></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s tc-ink"><div class="tc-w tc-ld"><div><p class="tc-ey">The questioning ladder</p><h2 class="rv">Climb from recall to creation</h2><p class="tc-p">Better questions make better thinking. Tap a rung for question starters you can use tomorrow.</p><div class="tc-stem" id="qs-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div><div class="tc-rungs" role="group" aria-label="Question levels"><button type="button" data-q="5"><span>06</span>Create</button><button type="button" data-q="4"><span>05</span>Evaluate</button><button type="button" data-q="3"><span>04</span>Analyse</button><button type="button" data-q="2"><span>03</span>Apply</button><button type="button" data-q="1"><span>02</span>Understand</button><button type="button" data-q="0" class="on"><span>01</span>Remember</button></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s" id="wait"><div class="tc-w tc-wt"><div class="tc-ring"><svg viewBox="0 0 220 220" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="110" cy="110" r="96" fill="none" stroke="#E7E6E0" stroke-width="10"/><circle id="wt-r" cx="110" cy="110" r="96" fill="none" stroke="#1F3BD1" stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round" transform="rotate(-90 110 110)" stroke-dasharray="603.2" stroke-dashoffset="603.2"/></svg><div class="tc-ring-t"><b id="wt-n">5</b><span id="wt-m">seconds</span></div></div><div><p class="tc-ey">Wait-time trainer</p><h2 class="rv">The most powerful pause in teaching</h2><p class="tc-p">After asking a question, many teachers wait less than a second. Waiting three to five seconds gives more students time to think, and answers get longer and better. Feel how long five seconds is.</p><button type="button" class="tc-btn tc-btn-s" id="wt-go"><span>Ask, then wait</span><i>&#9654;</i></button></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-por"><div class="tc-por-i pxw"><img data-px=".1" src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590650213165-c1fef80648c4?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1800&h=1000&q=75" alt="a smiling woman in a blue blazer leaning on a wall" width="1800" height="1000" loading="lazy"></div><blockquote class="tc-w rv"><p>&ldquo;The best teachers make the difficult feel <em>possible</em>.&rdquo;</p></blockquote></section>

<section class="tc-s"><div class="tc-w tc-still"><figure class="rv rv-c"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1503676260728-1c00da094a0b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&h=820&q=75" alt="a red apple on a stack of books" width="700" height="820" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="tc-ey">Small habits, big results</p><h2 class="rv">Six things to try this week</h2><ul class="tc-hab stg"><li class="rv">Start every lesson with three quick recall questions.</li><li class="rv">Write the objective in student-friendly words.</li><li class="rv">Use mini whiteboards so everyone answers.</li><li class="rv">Cold call kindly, after thinking time.</li><li class="rv">Say less; show more examples.</li><li class="rv">End with one exit question.</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s tc-idx-s"><div class="tc-w"><p class="tc-ey">The guides</p><ul class="tc-idx"><li><a href="lesson-planning-guide.html"><span>01</span><b>Lesson Planning</b><em>From objective to exit ticket</em><i>&rarr;</i></a></li><li><a href="classroom-management-guide.html"><span>02</span><b>Classroom Management</b><em>Routines, relationships, calm</em><i>&rarr;</i></a></li></ul></div></section>

